WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Speaker 1: Hey, get a load of this one. So North&nbsp;
Dakota's Senate recently passed a bill to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.540
the amount of money that state employees can&nbsp;
collect for meals while they're traveling on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.840
the job. Raised it from $35 to $45 a day. And&nbsp;
this is a benefit that lawmakers, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.520
will be able to enjoy as they are state employees&nbsp;
themselves. So it's a nice little perk that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.220
they've passed for themselves. And in a vacuum,&nbsp;
I really want to see a big issue over this. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.780
bill is going to cost taxpayers $1,000,000 over&nbsp;
the next two years, and it's now waiting for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:36.840
governor's signature to become law. Now, $45 a&nbsp;
day for food may sound like a lot. Sure. But you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.840
have to consider that if you're traveling,&nbsp;
you're not really going to be able to cook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.000
You're going to be eating out exclusively or&nbsp;
almost exclusively. So you think, okay, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:52.080
$10 for breakfast, $15 maybe for lunch, 20 bucks&nbsp;
for dinner. That adds up to 45. Okay. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.540
this makes sense. However, here is the rub. Less&nbsp;
than two weeks ago, the Senate in North Dakota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.060
rejected a separate bill that would have provided&nbsp;
$6 million over the next two years to give free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.420
school lunches to all K through 12 students&nbsp;
living at or below 200% of the federal poverty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.760
level. The bill failed by a single vote, with 13&nbsp;
Republicans voting against it, despite how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.920
backed this recent bill about reimbursement for&nbsp;
state employees. Not surprisingly, the Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.100
of course, backed the free school lunch bill.&nbsp;
The rejected school lunch bill aims to expand on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.040
an existing federal program that already provides&nbsp;
meals to students living below 130% of the poverty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.380
level. We're hearing some reactions from North&nbsp;
the North Dakota lawmakers about this, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.780
Senate Minority Leader Cathy Hogan, who's a&nbsp;
Democrat. She criticized the Senate for voting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.760
more free food for themselves while rejecting the&nbsp;
bill to provide food to hungry children. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.560
we're also hearing from Republicans like Assistant&nbsp;
Majority Leader Jerry Kline, who claims that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.720
he failed to see any correlation between the&nbsp;
two bills, which is laughable in my opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.300
because they both literally have to do with paying&nbsp;
for meals for people. One group of people being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.500
state officials and the other a group of people&nbsp;
being students and then get a load of this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.400
State Senator Mike Obama, Bama cited personal&nbsp;
responsibility for why kids shouldn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.280
free meals and blames negligent parents for the&nbsp;
problem. Yes, a lack of personal responsibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.140
among what? Kindergartners, first graders,&nbsp;
second graders. I mean, how does that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:31.620
any sense whatsoever? Real galaxy brained take&nbsp;
there. North Dakota has the second highest child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.260
poverty rate in the U.S. to put this in context.&nbsp;
And over 30% of its K through 12 students qualify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.960
for free or reduced price lunches. So this state,&nbsp;
more than almost every other state in the union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:50.100
is in need of a bill like this because we know the&nbsp;
importance of providing lunches to kids. It, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.840
course, ensures that they're going to have access&nbsp;
to adequate nutrition regardless of their ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:01.440
to pay. It will increase academic performance for&nbsp;
kids because it helps them focus on what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.600
learning in class instead of focusing on their&nbsp;
stomach rumbling. It helps with their development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.700
as they're, of course, still growing, and it&nbsp;
reduces stigma too. I remember when I was growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:16.320
up in school, we had these yellow lunch tickets.&nbsp;
If you were paying full price for the lunch, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:22.500
full $3.50 or whatever it was. But there were also&nbsp;
students who had the reduced price lunch tickets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.040
and those were a different color. Or maybe it&nbsp;
was orange, I don't exactly remember. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.500
was also the free lunch tickets, which I think&nbsp;
were maybe red. And so other students would see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.540
students pay with the yellow tickets or the red&nbsp;
tickets. The green tickets, and everyone would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:41.880
kind of have an assessment as to the financial&nbsp;
situation of the families of all these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.720
kids. And, you know, if you're one of the students&nbsp;
who are paying with the discounted or free lunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.740
tickets, there could easily be some stigma&nbsp;
there. Right? There could be some self-esteem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:56.040
issues that results from that, some mental health&nbsp;
troubles that result from that. And so in my mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.120
we should just do away with this altogether.&nbsp;
School lunches already incredibly subsidized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.400
and it's something that we as a nation can easily&nbsp;
afford. The pandemic era federal aid made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.920
school meals available for free to all public&nbsp;
school students. That ended on September 30th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.780
of last year. However, it's unfortunate that we&nbsp;
were not able to keep this thing going and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:21.960
it permanent one of the longer lasting results of&nbsp;
the pandemic. That would have been nice to see to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.480
just set this in stone and not go back on it. But&nbsp;
unfortunately, we are regressing on this issue and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.160
leaving it up to the states has not worked because&nbsp;
we're seeing what transpired here in North Dakota.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.360
And it really is too bad, especially seeing&nbsp;
the contradiction that we're seeing with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:43.080
totally fine approving, increasing the cost that&nbsp;
they're able to spend when it comes to meals for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:48.060
themselves, but not when it comes to. Students&nbsp;
in the most disadvantaged among us. All right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.900
That's going to do it for this segment of the&nbsp;
David Pakman show. But stick around, because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.980
have one more segment to go. We'll be back with&nbsp;
much more right after this commercial break.

